rows.aRaw:
Electrical discharge machining studies that specifically address Hastelloy indicate a marked sensitivity of process outcomes to the tool electrode material and its microstructure. Experimental comparisons using POCO graphite grades show that coarser graphite grains increase material removal rate (MRR) but at the cost of higher relative tool wear rate (TWR), whereas finer-grained graphite produces lower surface roughness (Ra). {{cite:S1#c1}} The same trend—improved efficiency and reduced wear with fine-grained graphite—was independently reported when EDM of Hastelloy C-22 was performed with negative polarity, and the authors linked detached graphite grains to instability in discharges and altered process efficiency. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Across the reviewed EDM literature the principal electrical variables controlling MRR and Ra are peak current (amperage) and pulse duration (pulse-on time), with discharge current often accounting for the largest fraction of variance in MRR and roughness metrics. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}} Studies on other difficult-to-machine alloys similarly identify current and pulse duration as dominant factors for TWR and surface deterioration, and experimental optimisation methods such as Taguchi and ANOVA are commonly applied to quantify these effects. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

Efforts to reduce TWR and improve dimensional accuracy in EDM use two broad strategies: modifying the electrode/workpiece physical state and altering the machining environment. Cryogenic treatment and process modifications such as powder-mixed dielectric or ultrasonic assistance are reported to change MRR, TWR and surface integrity by affecting thermal and electrical behaviours during sparking; meta-analyses of such associative techniques highlight their potential to mitigate the traditional trade-offs of EDM (high TWR, low MRR, coarse Ra). {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}} For electrodes specifically, treating or alloying electrode materials (example: treated brass) has produced large reductions in electrode wear in other workpieces, indicating electrode processing can be an effective route to lower EWR. {{cite:S7#c1}}

The literature also shows that EDM can be used for surface modification—electrical discharge coating (EDC)—introducing the possibility that electrode composition and deposited layers interact to affect surface integrity and coating characteristics. {{cite:S5#c1}} Studies on geometrical outcomes (for example square-hole machining of Hastelloy B2) emphasise that current and pulse duration not only drive wear rates but also change electrode geometry during machining, with sharp edges becoming rounded under unstable spark conditions. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Despite these findings, comparative data for copper electrodes and for engineered composite electrodes when machining Hastelloy alloys are not present in the assembled sources. [[NEEDS SOURCE: copper electrode performance on Hastelloy]] [[NEEDS SOURCE: composite electrode studies for Hastelloy]]

