### 

S1#c1 reports that, in EDM of Hastelloy C-22 with POCO graphite electrodes, the coarser-grained graphite (S-180, 10 µm) produced higher material removal rates but also greater relative tool wear, while the finer-grained graphite (AF-5, 1 µm) gave the lowest surface roughness; the study identified discharge current and pulse duration as the main drivers of MRR and Ra, and the time interval as the dominant factor for relative tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}}

S3#c1 finds that using fine-grained graphite in negative-polarity EDM of Hastelloy C-22 raises process efficiency and lowers tool wear, and that graphite grain detachment alters spark stability and process effectiveness; the authors produced mathematical models linking grain size and machining parameters to MRR, relative TWR, and Ra. {{cite:S3#c1}}

S2#c1 shows that, for a corrosion-resistant superalloy tested under varied pulse-on times (300–500 μs) and peak currents (6 and 10 A), peak current dominated both average surface roughness and MRR in ANOVA (74.79% and 86.43% influence, respectively), and Taguchi–gray relational analysis identified cryogenic treatment, pulse-on time and peak current as key factors for combined Ra and MRR performance. {{cite:S2#c1}}

S4#c1 measured EDM of Hastelloy B2 and observed that current and pulse duration strongly affect electrode wear, producing rapid rounding of square-electrode edges into hemispherical ends because unstable sparks and heat diffusion remove sharp features. {{cite:S4#c1}} S8#c1 similarly quantifies amperage as the overwhelmingly dominant factor for MRR and TWR in titanium EDM experiments, reporting percentage contributions from ANOVA that place amperage above other factors for multiple performance metrics. {{cite:S8#c1}}

S6#c1 summarizes that researchers apply various auxiliary methods—cryogenic treatment, cryogenic cooling, powder-mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance—to reduce high TWR, low MRR and poor surface quality inherent to EDM, and it evaluates these associations against measures such as MRR, TWR, surface integrity and recast layer. {{cite:S6#c1}} Experimental results for brass electrodes in nanopowder-mixed and cryogenically treated conditions demonstrate large reductions in electrode wear rate and improvements in dimensional accuracy when deionized water or kerosene are used as dielectrics, with cryogenic treatment providing the largest EWR decreases reported in that study. {{cite:S7#c1}}

S5#c1 reviews electrical discharge coating (EDC) as a related process that deposits hard coatings via discharges and summarizes key EDC parameters and coating characteristics, implying a potential route to modify surface properties produced by discharge-based processes. {{cite:S5#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: Comparative data for copper and composite electrodes on Hastelloy]]

