### 

Studies that directly compare electrode materials in EDM of nickel-based alloys focus predominantly on graphite and on process parameters that govern both material removal rate (MRR) and tool wear rate (TWR). Experimental work on Hastelloy C-22 shows that graphite electrode grain size substantially alters machining outcomes: coarser-grained graphite yields higher MRR but also increases relative tool wear, whereas finer-grained graphite produces lower surface roughness and, in later work, is reported to increase process efficiency while reducing tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Multiple investigations identify the same set of machining variables as primary drivers of MRR, TWR and surface finish. For EDM of Hastelloy, discharge current (peak current) and pulse duration emerge repeatedly as the dominant factors controlling MRR and Ra, while the interval between pulses (time off) is particularly influential for relative tool wear of graphite electrodes. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} In a factorial study that included cryogenic treatments, peak current accounted for the largest share of variance in both MRR and average surface roughness, consistent with the central role of current in thermal removal mechanisms. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Work on other nickel alloys reinforces the sensitivity of electrode geometry and wear to current and pulse duration: experiments on Hastelloy B2 reported that higher current and longer pulses substantially affect electrode taper, end radius and wear length, often rounding sharp electrode features into hemispherical ends through unstable sparking and heat diffusion. {{cite:S4#c1}} More generally, literature reviews of EDM note that the method’s main limitations are elevated TWR, low MRR relative to some processes, and degraded surface integrity from persistent sparking; reviewers compile various mitigation strategies such as cryogenic treatment, cryogenic cooling, powder-mixed dielectrics, and ultrasonic assistance to address these problems. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Alternate process variants and electrode treatments show promise for reducing wear or improving surface outcomes. Nanopowder-mixed EDM combined with cryogenic-treated electrodes produced substantial reductions in electrode wear rate and overcut for aluminium and brass electrodes in the reviewed experiments, illustrating how dielectric additives and electrode conditioning can change EWR and dimensional accuracy. {{cite:S7#c1}} Electrical discharge coating (EDC) is presented as a related technique to modify surface characteristics or deposit hard coatings via discharges, offering another route to tailor surface integrity after EDM. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Despite these findings, comparative data for copper electrodes on Hastelloy are not presented in the sourced literature. [[NEEDS SOURCE: comparative data for copper electrodes on Hastelloy]] Similarly, the literature supplied contains no direct experimental comparisons involving composite electrodes on Hastelloy. [[NEEDS SOURCE: comparative data for composite electrodes on Hastelloy]]

