### Electrical discharge machining performance with fine- and coarse-grained graphite electrodes

Nowicki et al. 2022 report that graphite with larger grains yields higher material removal rate but also produces greater relative tool wear when machining Hastelloy C-22 with EDM. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
Their experiments compared POCO AF-5 (1 µm) and S-180 (10 µm) graphite and identified discharge current and pulse duration as the principal controls of MRR and Ra for both graphites, while the time interval (pulse-off) dominated relative tool wear of the graphite electrode. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
The same pair of graphite grades was studied again by Nowicki et al. 2024 under negative polarity, where fine-grained POCO AF-5 increased process efficiency and reduced tool wear compared with coarse-grained EDM-180. {{cite:S3#c1}}  
That study additionally observed that detached graphite grains alter discharge stability and thereby affect process efficiency, and it produced mathematical models linking machining parameters to MRR, relative TWR and Ra for Hastelloy C-22. {{cite:S3#c1}}

### Process factors driving MRR, TWR and surface quality across studies

Peak current is reported as the dominant single parameter for both MRR and average surface roughness in the Taguchi/ANOVA analysis performed on a corrosion-resistant superalloy: it accounted for 86.43% of variance in MRR and 74.79% in Ra in Nas & Kara 2022. {{cite:S2#c1}}  
Akincioglu 2022 likewise found amperage (current) to be the most influential factor on MRR and TWR in EDM of titanium, with percentages near or above 99% in their ANOVA results. {{cite:S8#c1}}  
Ramabalan et al. 2024 show that current and pulse duration significantly affect electrode wear morphology during machining of Hastelloy B2, producing transformation of sharp square-electrode ends into hemispherical shapes under unstable sparking. {{cite:S4#c1}}  
Shastri et al. 2022 synthesise a range of mitigation strategies (cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed EDM, ultrasonic assistance) aimed at reducing high TWR, low MRR and poor surface integrity commonly observed in EDM processes. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### Alternative electrode and process interventions reported

Cryogenic treatment of workpieces and electrodes appears in experimental programmes: Nas & Kara 2022 identify an optimal combination including shallow cryogenic treatment, low pulse-on time and low peak current for combined Ra and MRR objectives in their Taguchi-gray analysis. {{cite:S2#c1}}  
Nanopowder in the dielectric together with electrode cryogenic treatment was reported to lower electrode wear and overcut for brass electrodes in a study using ANN-MOGA optimisation, with cryogenically treated brass showing the largest reductions in EWR and dimensional error when deionized water was used as the dielectric. {{cite:S7#c1}}  
Liew et al. 2020 review electrical discharge coating (EDC) as a route to modify surface properties via discharges, summarising process parameters and coating characteristics relevant where surface integrity or new surface functions are targeted. {{cite:S5#c1}}

