Selective laser melting (SLM) produces near‑fully dense metallic parts by layerwise laser melting of powder, and the process intrinsically generates unique, fine microstructures through very high cooling rates. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} The rapid thermal cycles in SLM act as an "in situ" heat treatment that promotes hierarchical (sub)grain structures and fine precipitates, features that distinguish additively manufactured steels from conventionally produced counterparts. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Studies on 18Ni‑300 (grade 300) maraging steel fabricated by SLM report an as‑built microstructure dominated by a very fine martensitic matrix and extremely small grains, consistent with cooling rates on the order of $10^7\ \mathrm{K/s}$. {{cite:S3#c1}} Electron microscopy shows abundant needle‑shaped nanoprecipitates of Ni$_3$X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) embedded in the martensite, which are implicated in age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Other work characterises the as‑printed condition of SLM maraging steel as a fine‑grained martensitic structure with relatively high surface roughness and no strong crystallographic texture across orthogonal sections. {{cite:S8#c1}} These as‑built features—fine martensite, submicron grain size, and pre‑existing nanoscale precipitates—explain the high strength but can contribute to reduced ductility without appropriate post‑processing. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}

Post‑processing modifies those microstructural states: heat treatments relieve residual stresses, promote precipitation strengthening, and can be combined with thermo‑chemical treatments. {{cite:S3#c1}} Aging produces a maximum in nano‑hardness and correlates with precipitation evolution, while solution treatments and aging influence the response to subsequent nitriding. {{cite:S3#c1}} In one study, plasma nitriding performed at $520\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ for 6 h produced a compound layer whose integrity depended on prior heat treatment; nitriding without prior aging induced cracks in the nitride layer, whereas nitriding after prior heat treatment improved both wear and corrosion resistance relative to untreated additive counterparts. {{cite:S2#c1}} The combination of SLM’s intrinsic thermal history and controlled post‑treatments therefore governs the balance between mechanical strengthening and surface integrity after thermo‑chemical processing. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}}

Reversed austenite formed by intercritical tempering has a measurable effect on chloride‑induced corrosion. {{cite:S4#c1}} Khan et al. tempered SLM maraging steel at intercritical temperatures near $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ to induce austenite reversion along martensite lath boundaries and found that the reversed austenite was associated with local Ni enrichment and improved pitting resistance in $3.5\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ NaCl: higher pitting potentials and lower corrosion current densities were reported for the $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$‑tempered specimens compared with as‑printed, aged, and homogenized samples. {{cite:S4#c1}} Comparisons between SLM and wrought maraging steel after heat treatment show similar nano‑hardness and corrosion behaviour in the studied heat‑treated states, with precipitation playing a primary role in both hardness and corrosion potential; electropolishing can substantially reduce surface roughness and thereby affect measured corrosion responses. {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Tan et al. observe an average submicron grain size of $0.31\ \mu\text{m}$ in SLM 300 maraging steel, implying cooling rates up to $10^7\ \text{K/s}$ in the as-fabricated material. {{cite:S3#c1}} This rapid solidification produces a martensitic matrix containing dense, needle-shaped Ni$_3$X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) nanoprecipitates that account for age hardening, and it also generates residual stress and building-orientation anisotropy; subsequent heat treatments both promote precipitation that strengthens the material and reduce residual stress while partially alleviating anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} The review of AM steels further links these unique, layerwise microstructural features—hierarchical (sub)grains and fine precipitates—to enhanced strength and modified corrosion response relative to conventionally produced steels. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Ahmadkhaniha et al. report a fine-grained martensitic microstructure in as-printed maraging steel with a surface roughness $R_a\approx 10\ \mu\text{m}$ and no detectable preferred texture across xy, xz and yz sections. {{cite:S8#c1}} Their measurements show aging produces the maximum nano-hardness and the lowest corrosion potential among the tested conditions, and they attribute both hardness and corrosion behaviour primarily to precipitation phenomena; electropolishing reduced $R_a$ by about 65\%. {{cite:S8#c1}} These findings indicate that surface condition and precipitation state co-control both mechanical and electrochemical responses in SLM maraging steel. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Khan et al. find that an intercritical temper at $720\ ^\circ\text{C}$ produces reversed austenite that localises mainly along martensite-lath boundaries via Ni enrichment from dissolved intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} Electrochemical testing in $3.5\ \text{wt}\%$ NaCl shows the $720\ ^\circ\text{C}$-tempered specimens possess a higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, or homogenized samples lacking reversed austenite, so the study concludes that reversed austenite improves pitting resistance in chloride media. {{cite:S4#c1}} Khan et al. also note that, before their work, the effect of martensite-to-austenite reversion on corrosion had been insufficiently understood. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Godec et al. report that plasma nitriding performed without prior aging produces cracks in the compound nitride layer, whereas nitriding applied after appropriate prior heat treatments yields improved wear and corrosion resistance in SLM 18Ni-300 relative to untreated AM counterparts. {{cite:S2#c1}} Together with broader reviews of SLM, these studies indicate that the AM process itself induces in situ thermal histories and precipitate distributions that strongly interact with subsequent heat- and thermochemical treatments to determine corrosion performance. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: systematic comparisons across chloride concentrations and durations]]

