Selective laser melting (SLM) builds metallic parts by layerwise laser melting and fusion of powder, producing near-net-shape components with relative densities approaching 99.9% under optimized conditions. {{cite:S1#c1}} The layerwise thermal cycling and high local cooling rates in laser powder bed fusion create microstructures and precipitate states that differ from conventionally processed steels and can enhance properties such as strength, wear and corrosion resistance while posing challenges for ductility and fatigue. {{cite:S6#c1}}

In the as-built condition, SLM maraging steel exhibits an extremely fine martensitic structure consistent across build sections and without a pronounced crystallographic texture. {{cite:S8#c1}} High cooling rates during SLM produce very small submicron grains (reported average $0.31\ \mu\text{m}$) and promote the formation of dense arrays of needle-shaped nanoprecipitates of Ni$_3$X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) within the martensitic matrix, features that underpin the material’s age-hardening response. {{cite:S3#c1}} Surface finish in the as-printed state can be relatively coarse (reported Ra ≈ 10 µm) but may be changed substantially by post-processing such as electropolishing. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Post-build thermal treatments alter both the fine-scale precipitate state and residual stress. Systematic aging informed by detected phase-transformation temperatures produces extensive Ni$_3$X precipitation that increases hardness and relieves residual stresses, and reduces build-orientation mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Thermochemical routes applied after SLM—such as plasma nitriding—interact with the prior heat-treatment condition: nitriding without prior aging produced defects in the compound layer, whereas nitriding of specimens that had been heat treated improved wear and corrosion resistance relative to untreated additive-manufactured counterparts. {{cite:S2#c1}} The AM process itself can act as an intrinsic heat treatment, causing in situ tempering and precipitation phenomena that influence the final microstructure and properties. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Intercritical tempering that induces martensite-to-austenite reversion (reversed austenite) has been linked to changes in corrosion performance in chloride media. In SLM maraging steel tempered within the $\alpha' + \gamma$ range (example: 720°C), reversed austenite nucleates preferentially at martensite lath boundaries where local Ni enrichment occurs, and specimens with such reversed austenite exhibited higher pitting potentials, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rates in 3.5 wt% NaCl than as-printed, conventionally aged, or homogenized samples without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}} More generally, precipitation state governs both hardness and electrochemical behaviour: aging that maximizes nano-hardness also shifts corrosion potential, indicating that precipitate evolution strongly affects corrosion response. {{cite:S8#c1}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: quantitative comparison of pitting morphologies and pit growth rates]]

rows.bRaw:
Tan et al. report an average submicron grain size of $0.31\ \mu$m in SLM 18Ni-300 maraging steel, indicating cooling rates on the order of $10^{7}\ \mathrm{K/s}$ during fabrication. {{cite:S3#c1}} This high cooling rate produces a fine martensitic matrix containing dense, needle-shaped Ni3X precipitates that form in situ and contribute to age hardening; microscopy in that study resolved interfacial relationships between precipitates and the matrix and linked strengthening to Orowan and coherency-strain mechanisms. {{cite:S3#c1}} Haghdadi et al. synthesize findings across AM steels and attribute such hierarchical subgrain structures and fine precipitates to the unique thermal cycles of laser powder bed fusion, noting that these microstructural features often yield higher strength and modified corrosion behaviour relative to conventionally produced steels. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Ahmadkhaniha et al. find that as-printed SLM maraging steel exhibits a fine-grained martensitic structure with a surface roughness $Ra\approx 10\ \mu$m and no strong crystallographic texture across three orthogonal sections. {{cite:S8#c1}} In their comparison, nano-hardness and corrosion behaviour of heat-treated SLM specimens and conventionally wrought maraging steel are similar, and aging produces the highest nano-hardness while lowering corrosion potential; the study identifies precipitation as the principal factor controlling both hardness and corrosion response. {{cite:S8#c1}} Yap et al. describe SLM as capable of producing near-full-density parts (up to 99.9% relative density) through layerwise laser melting and rapid solidification, which underpins the dense microstructures observed in these experimental reports. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Khan et al. report that tempering in the intercritical $\alpha' + \gamma$ range at $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ produces reversed austenite that precipitates primarily along martensite-lath boundaries in SLM maraging steel, a process promoted by local nickel enrichment from dissolving intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} That study measured electrochemical behaviour in $3.5\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ NaCl and found the $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ tempered specimens show a higher pitting potential, reduced corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate compared with as-printed, aged, and homogenized specimens lacking reversed austenite; the authors conclude that reversed austenite improves pitting resistance. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Godec et al. examined plasma nitriding applied to SLM 18Ni-300 after varied prior heat treatments and observed that nitriding without prior aging produced cracks in the compound layer, whereas nitriding following prior heat treatment improved wear and corrosion resistance of the additive-manufactured material. {{cite:S2#c1}} Yan et al. compared SLM and cold-spray composites and showed SLM yields higher densification and lower porosity, factors that influence both mechanical behaviour and likely surface-controlled corrosion processes. {{cite:S7#c1}} Afkhami et al. and Haghdadi et al. highlight remaining gaps: although AM induces in situ tempering and precipitation that modify corrosion response, acceptable ductility and fatigue remain challenges and the interplay between microstructural anisotropy, heat treatment routes, and chloride-induced corrosion is not comprehensively mapped. {{cite:S5#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: quantitative chloride pitting thresholds across heat treatments]]

