Household decisions about rooftop photovoltaic (PV) adoption are strongly shaped by financial considerations: initial capital outlay and perceptions of running costs form persistent inhibitors. Empirical analyses identify upfront cost and expected maintenance burden as negative influences on willingness to adopt, with cost often appearing as a primary barrier in survey-based studies. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Several studies find that finance design and payment flexibility mitigate affordability constraints and thereby raise uptake among lower-income or rural households. A nationally representative analysis from Uganda shows that loan- or installment-style payment options increase uptake of larger solar home systems relative to one-time purchase, implying that flexible finance converts constrained demand into actual adoption. {{cite:S7#c1}} Parsad et al. likewise report that subsidies reduce initial investment risk and operate as a key financial motivator in Indian household decisions. {{cite:S3#c1}} 

Policy instruments and subsidy design therefore play an outsized role in shaping household investor behavior, both by lowering up-front risk and by signaling governmental support. Indian policy goals and targets frame these incentives: national targets for solar capacity position rooftop PV as a deliberate element of the renewable portfolio, and analyses of Indian policy instruments link these targets to the broader effort to expand rooftop deployment. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S5#c1}} Specific pilot implementations also reveal gaps between policy intent and on-the-ground uptake, indicating that incentives alone do not guarantee diffusion. {{cite:S8#c1}} 

Non-financial constraints interact with financial barriers: limited technical knowledge, weak awareness, and perceived risks reduce adoption even where subsidies or finance are available. Parsad et al. find that technical barriers and deficits in awareness remain significant predictors of investment decisions, and that non-financial motivators such as environmental concern operate alongside but do not fully substitute for clear information and trust in the technology. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} Field surveys in pilot projects underscore user-experience issues and information gaps about site suitability and system performance as impediments to uptake. {{cite:S8#c1}} 

Equity and spatial patterns further complicate adoption dynamics: research warns that growth in household solar can produce uneven outcomes across demographic and geographic groups, so that expanding deployment without attention to distributional effects risks reinforcing inequities. {{cite:S4#c1}} At the same time, country-level reviews highlight infrastructural and institutional constraints that shape the feasibility of scaling rooftop PV, meaning that technical potential alone does not resolve barriers embedded in governance, market structure, and program implementation. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S5#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Parsad et al. 2020 find that government subsidies are the primary motivator that helps households overcome the initial investment risk for rooftop PV, and that low purchase prices and low maintenance costs further strengthen the financial case for adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}} Paring structural-equation outputs with an artificial neural network, they report that technical barriers and gaps in knowledge and awareness also significantly forecast investment decisions, indicating that policy support interacts with informational and technical constraints. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Aarakit et al. 2021 find that affordability governs which solar products households choose: flexible payment mechanisms increase uptake of more expensive solar home systems and kits, while rural residence and income predict the type of system adopted. {{cite:S7#c1}} From this they infer that absence of payment flexibility constrains poorer households from selecting higher-capacity rooftop solutions. {{cite:S7#c1}} Tanveer et al. 2021 similarly report that perceived cost negatively affects willingness to adopt household PV, while perceived benefits and self-efficacy increase willingness. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Kappagantu et al. 2015 report survey evidence from a smart-grid pilot in Puducherry showing that users raise questions about available roof space, experience with the technology, and practical installation concerns. {{cite:S8#c1}} Parsad et al. 2020 identify technical barriers alongside knowledge and awareness deficits as significant inhibitors in household decision models, suggesting that practical site constraints and limited user familiarity reduce adoption propensity. {{cite:S3#c1}} These findings together indicate that both physical feasibility on individual homes and gaps in consumer understanding limit rooftop deployment. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

Goel 2016 and J & Majid 2020 document that India has explicit national targets and policy instruments aimed at scaling rooftop solar, but they also highlight a set of implementation challenges and obstacles that complicate meeting those targets. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}} Kappagantu et al. 2015 discuss the on-the-ground effects of government initiatives within the Puducherry pilot, reporting survey results that illuminate how programme design and local implementation shape consumer responses. {{cite:S8#c1}} Together these studies indicate that institutional and policy barriers are not solely absence of ambition but include programme design, rollout, and interaction with household-level constraints. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

Sovacool et al. 2022 find that household solar expansion can generate multiple inequities — demographic, spatial, interspecies and temporal — and that these justice dimensions intersect with deployment choices at neighbourhood and supply-chain scales. {{cite:S4#c1}} Where studies of uptake emphasise affordability and payment options, Sovacool et al. show that distributional outcomes and long-term equity implications also need attention when interpreting barriers to household adoption. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: empirical estimates of upfront cost magnitudes in India]]

