Rooftop solar adoption decisions are shaped by a mix of financial and non‑financial factors that studies identify as interlinked barriers and motivators. Several papers report that upfront cost and concerns about long‑term returns are central financial inhibitors: households perceive initial investment risk and ongoing maintenance costs as obstacles to adopting PV systems, while subsidies and low prices emerge as primary policy and market levers that lower that perceived economic risk. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} Evidence from mixed‑method and survey studies therefore treats subsidy design and price reductions as direct mechanisms to improve uptake by changing households’ risk–return calculations. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Affordability and payment flexibility recur as decisive determinants in lower‑income and rural contexts. A nationally representative analysis from Uganda finds that affordability governs which type of solar technology households choose, and that flexible payment mechanisms meaningfully increase uptake among income‑constrained households; the same study reports that access to grid electricity is negatively associated with uptake of off‑grid solar kits and home systems. {{cite:S7#c1}} These results underscore that financing structures — not merely sticker prices — shape adoption patterns in settings where many households face liquidity constraints. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Non‑financial barriers such as limited knowledge, technical concerns, and institutional frictions also constrain adoption and interact with economic barriers. Survey‑based work in India shows that technical barriers and deficits in knowledge and awareness significantly predict household investment decisions in rooftop PV, even after accounting for financial motivators like subsidies. {{cite:S3#c1}} Complementary field surveys in an Indian smart‑grid pilot examined consumer experience, roof‑space constraints, and responses to government initiatives, documenting implementation challenges at the consumer level that reflect both informational gaps and practical site limitations. {{cite:S8#c1}}

At the policy and equity level, broader reviews and mixed‑methods studies highlight spatial and social justice dimensions that complicate uniform policy responses. A literature‑driven study combining interviews and neighbourhood site visits maps four types of inequities — demographic, spatial, interspecies and temporal — that can be amplified as household solar scales, indicating that policy design must account for distributional outcomes as well as aggregate capacity targets. {{cite:S4#c1}} National policy ambitions and targets contextualize these distributional concerns: analyses of India’s solar targets and policy instruments describe ambitious capacity goals for rooftop PV alongside persistent challenges in translating national targets into widespread household adoption. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S5#c1}}

Taken together, the literature frames rooftop PV diffusion as a problem of coupled constraints: financial affordability and subsidy design, payment modalities, awareness and technical confidence, and institutional or policy implementation issues each play distinct roles and frequently interact. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: quantitative comparison of barrier magnitudes across regions]]

rows.bRaw:
Goel 2016 reports that national targets assign 40 GW of the planned 100 GW solar objective to grid‑connected rooftop PV, situating rooftop systems as a central element of India’s solar expansion goals. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Parsad et al. 2020 find that government subsidies function as the main motivator by reducing the initial investment risk for households, and that lower purchase prices and reduced maintenance costs further encourage investment. {{cite:S3#c1}} Parsad et al. 2020 also identify technical barriers and gaps in knowledge and awareness as significant inhibitors in forecasting household investment decisions. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Tanveer et al. 2021 report that higher perceived cost and greater perceived risk both reduce consumers’ willingness to adopt household PV, while social norms and self‑efficacy increase willingness to adopt. {{cite:S6#c1}} The same study finds that openness to technology did not have a statistically significant effect on adoption in its sample. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Aarakit et al. 2021 show that affordability determines both whether households adopt solar PV and the type of system they select, with flexible payment mechanisms increasing uptake among income‑constrained households. {{cite:S7#c1}} Their analysis further indicates that uptake is concentrated in rural areas and that access to the grid is negatively associated with adoption of small solar kits and home systems. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Kappagantu et al. 2015 present survey evidence from a smart‑grid pilot that documents variation in roof space availability and in users’ prior experience with rooftop PV among electricity consumers, factors that affect practical feasibility and user readiness for installation. {{cite:S8#c1}} Sovacool et al. 2022 complement these findings by identifying multiple equity dimensions—demographic, spatial, interspecies, and temporal—that can create distributional and procedural barriers as household solar deployment scales. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Khare et al. 2013 frame these barriers within broader system constraints by noting that technical and institutional obstacles persist despite adequate solar resource potential, implying that natural resource availability alone does not translate into household uptake. {{cite:S2#c1}} J & Majid 2020 review similar impediments at the policy and market level and highlight that targeted recommendations are needed to overcome the obstacles identified in the sectoral literature. {{cite:S5#c1}}

