Agarwal et al. 2018 found no difference between intervention and control arms in HbA1c at 3 months in a multicentre pragmatic trial of a diabetes self-management app, although exploratory analysis linked greater individual app use to small reductions in HbA1c. {{cite:S6#c1}} The trial reported a mean difference (ITG−WLC) of −0.42 at 3 months that was not statistically significant, and an exploratory within-user finding that each additional day of app use corresponded to a $0.016$-point lower HbA1c at three months (95% CI $-0.03$ to $-0.003$). {{cite:S6#c1}} The same study also documented wide variation in engagement by clinical site, with median days of use ranging from 9 to 36 over 14 weeks, indicating that context influenced real-world uptake. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Zhao et al. 2016 reported that, across 23 app-based interventions for diverse health behaviours, 17 studies observed statistically significant behaviour change and many interventions achieved retention rates of 60%–100% in intervention groups. {{cite:S4#c1}} Their synthesis also identified common app features associated with effectiveness such as real-time feedback, individualized content, user-friendly design, low time burden, and involvement of health professionals. {{cite:S4#c1}} However, Zhao et al. judged all included studies as having some risk of bias and called for larger, higher-quality trials. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Cafazzo et al. 2012 found that gamification and automated data transfer increased the daily frequency of blood glucose measurement by 50% in a 12-week pilot of adolescents, with satisfaction high and many participants saying they would continue use. {{cite:S2#c1}} That pilot tied rewards to monitoring frequency and provided immediate trend analysis with prompts for causes and remedies, but the authors cautioned that improving HbA1c would require incentives linked to decision-making and actions following readings. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Peng et al. 2016 identified nine user-centred themes that shape adoption and continuation, including barriers, motivators, personalized information, tracking for awareness, credibility, goal setting, reminders, and concerns about sharing personal data. {{cite:S5#c1}} These themes map onto design choices shown elsewhere to influence sustained use, such as automated data capture and offline functionality. {{cite:S5#c1}} Izahar et al. 2017 found that many commercial diabetes apps emphasise reporting and reminders over personalised education or therapeutic support, with only a minority scoring highly across eight self-management features. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Jeffrey et al. 2019 reported that users perceive visual trend displays, intuitive navigation and convenience as facilitators, while lack of clinician endorsement, limited awareness, and low health or technical literacy act as barriers; participants recommended more educational, monitoring and nutritional features. {{cite:S8#c1}} Boulos et al. 2014 highlighted the very large and heterogeneous app market and concerns about quality, arguing that content quality, usability, device connectivity, security and literacy-matching are essential ingredients beyond medical-device regulation. {{cite:S3#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: randomized HbA1c outcomes from large type 2 diabetes app trials]]

